phases:
- post 3e - delete Banks and old Map read methods, transitional self.mapped
  branch, fold mem.rs into memory.rs
- phase 4 - mapper ops and trait diet -- DONE
  - MapperOps (3e8062a), Map trait diet (da150b1), boards! table (f40c729),
    stable serialization ids (1910ed8). Net -2.2% on the corpus, which paid
    back the MapperOps regression.
- phase 5 - save-state split -- DONE. Memory serializes only data[ram_start..];
  Cpu::load reattaches ROM from the running console and rejects a state from
  another cart (cpu::StateMismatch). SMB3 state 417KB -> 23KB (-94.5%),
  rewind buffer ~375MB -> ~21MB, run-ahead 0.659ms -> 0.051ms per frame.
  clock_frame neutral. SAVE_VERSION stays 2 - still undeployed, same breaking
  change as the mapper ids.
  - Follow-up: run-ahead now snapshots with Cpu::clone instead of bincode
    (1.6-5.1x faster round trip; 21x vs pre-phase-5). Rewind deliberately
    keeps the serialized form - it holds ~900 snapshots at once.
  - Benchmarking: there is NO directory/tmpfs effect (same commit from
    btrfs/tmpfs/long paths builds a byte-identical binary). The real effect
    is that the first run after a rebuild is ~1.5% slow. Run it twice, use
    the second. benches/README.md has the experiment.
- phase 6 - clean up public apis -- DONE
  - Collapsed 10 clocking entry points to 4. clock_frame is the one to use and
    honors set_run_ahead/Config::run_ahead; frame_buffer/frame_buffer_into/
    audio_samples report what it produced. clock_frame_output/into/ahead/
    ahead_into/clock_seconds_output are #[deprecated] shims forwarding to it.
  - Run-ahead used to be visible ONLY through its closure: it rewinds the
    console past the displayed frame, so frame_buffer() afterwards returned the
    pre-run-ahead frame. RunAheadFrames now parks the future frame's pixels
    (pending + pending_frame_number) and lends the PPU a recycled spare, so the
    accessors see the right frame. Regression test:
    run_ahead_reports_the_future_frame_not_the_rewound_one.
  - The mem::swap also drops the per-frame 120 KiB zeroing alloc that
    mem::take's Default did: run_ahead=1 is 5.245 -> 5.064 ms/frame (-3.4%).
    Plain clock_frame 2.593 -> 2.556 (neutral, inside noise). New
    TETANES_BENCH_RUN_AHEAD=n gives that path bench coverage it never had.
  - Audio no longer accumulates: clocking clears the previous call's samples
    first. Opt out with Config::clear_audio_on_clock=false when you clock
    several times before draining - which is what the ROM audio-test path in
    common.rs now does. Clear is once per OUTER call, so 2x speed still reports
    both NES frames; run-ahead's intermediate frames are always discarded since
    they belong to a rewound timeline.
  - Fixed: apu_mut returned &Apu, joypad took &mut self, clock_frame_ahead_into
    never set skip_rendering, clock_frame_into panicked when the caller's buffer
    was longer than the frame, cpu_corrupted was #[cold] AND #[inline(always)],
    ControlDeck::wram returned &[u8] where Bus::wram returns &[u8; WRAM].
  - Deprecated: pub mod error (entirely dead), common::SAVE_DIR/SRAM_DIR,
    channel_enabled (-> apu_channel_enabled). hexdump KEPT for the planned
    debugger hex viewer, now with docs + a doctest.
  - Docs: 2 doctests -> 14, rewritten README example, control_deck module doc,
    and the "adding a mapper" guide now lives in mapper.rs's module doc.
  - typos (crate-ci) added: _typos.toml, cargo make typos (in the lint chain),
    and .github/workflows/spelling.yml. Deliberately NOT a job in ci.yml, which
    has a workflow-level paths-ignore "**.md" - markdown is where prose typos
    live. Excludes docs/ and vendored/ (verbatim third-party) and the generated
    CHANGELOGs; extend-words covers NES jargon (BA bit-fields, ANC/SHS opcodes,
    romanized ROM titles).
  - Downstream survey: only 2 real third-party users of tetanes-core. nesbox/
    dappicom/zk-nintendo all vendor the PRE-SPLIT tetanes 0.8/0.9 crate and are
    unaffected. djanatyn/chaos-seeker uses ControlDeck normally from crates.io
    0.12.2 - the deprecations cover it. tedsteen/nes-bundler (166*, active)
    pins a GIT REV so it sees no deprecation warnings at all, and bypasses
    ControlDeck entirely (drives Cpu/Bus, reimplements clock_frame + run-ahead);
    it is ALREADY broken by phases 4/5 in six places (deleted traits, mem ->
    memory, Memory::new signature, Bus::sram, Apu::clock_flush, ppu.bus.mapper).
    TODO: changelog migration section + open an issue on nes-bundler. Do NOT
    re-add those internals; that would undo phases 4/5.

- phase 6 follow-ups -- DONE
  - MSRV 1.85 -> 1.88 (Cargo.toml, README, CLAUDE.md, ci.yml matrix). 1.85 could
    not build the crate AT ALL - let-chains in ppu.rs need 1.88 - so that CI job
    had been red for a while. Stable was red too (collapsible_if in
    m176_fk23c.rs); fixed as a match guard, which is equivalent here because the
    guard falls through to `_` and still reaches the trailing update_banks().
    bandai_fcg.rs got a let-chain and hexdump uses is_multiple_of, both now that
    1.88 allows it. nightly/stable/1.88 all green.
  - Frame buffers are sized: Ppu::frame_buffer and ControlDeck::frame_buffer_raw
    return &[u16; ppu::size::FRAME]; ControlDeck::frame_buffer returns
    &[u8; Frame::SIZE]; frame_buffer_into and all of Video's filter fns take
    &mut [u8; Frame::SIZE]. Callers no longer have to guess a length and the doc
    comments lost a paragraph each. Frame::as_array/as_array_mut are the bridge
    from the Vec-backed Frame - they carry the one try_into().expect(), which
    phase 8 removes by making Frame hold a boxed array.
  - Map::sync -> Map::update_banks, Ppu::sync_mapper -> rebuild_mapper_state,
    and the per-board helpers went with them: sync_prg/sync_chr/sync_nametables/
    sync_ex_ram -> update_prg_banks/update_chr_banks/update_nametables/
    update_ex_ram. So every board now reads update_banks -> update_*_banks.
    The one exception is Exrom, which HAD an update_chr_banks(memory, force)
    that collided. It was misnamed: it is not a banking function but the policy
    layer ABOVE sync_chr, and it exists only because MMC5 is the sole board with
    two CHR register sets ('A' sprite / 'B' background) that it swaps between
    mid-frame as the PPU alternates fetches. Everyone else maps as a pure
    function of one register set. Renamed to select_chr_set, which is what it
    does - compares the required set against the active one and re-maps only on
    a change (it is on the hot PPU-fetch path); `force` is for callers that just
    rewrote the registers.
  - TETANES_BENCH_OUTPUT is now TETANES_BENCH_NO_OUTPUT, i.e. filtering is ON by
    default, since every real frame is filtered. Measured cost: 2.556 -> 2.708
    ms/frame, +5.9%. Use NO_OUTPUT=1 when A/B-ing a core change, where that
    constant offset just dilutes the delta. EVERY baseline in benches/README.md
    predates this flip and excluded the filter - compare those against
    NO_OUTPUT=1 runs only.

- phase 7 - ROM test debt -- DONE. ZERO ignored tests left in the crate, from
  21 ignored ROM tests plus 9 never-counted bus::test stubs. Suite 217 -> 319.
  Three latent VRC6 overflow panics fixed; 64 new mapper unit tests over 8
  previously-uncovered boards. See below for what each item found.
- phase 8 - deferred API work (PLANNED, see below)

